From: SkiffOS <skiffos@skiffos.com>
Date: Fri, 04 Apr 2026 14:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] HACK: arm: kernel: sun5i AHB/MBUS settle after BSS zero in __mmap_switched

On Allwinner sun5i (R8/A13, C.H.I.P.) the kernel is completely silent
after the zImage decompressor jumps to the decompressed kernel, even
with settles in __enter_kernel (compressed/head.S) and at the end of
__create_page_tables (head.S).

Root cause: in __mmap_switched (head-common.S), __memset zeros ~1.6 MiB
of BSS to DRAM in a single sustained burst.  The AHB/MBUS bus arbiter
retains those write transactions; the very first post-BSS DRAM read
(adr_l r0, init_task) stalls the CPU indefinitely.

Fix: after bl __memset returns and before adr_l r0, init_task, issue a
DSB + busy-wait loop to drain the arbiter.

  mcr p15, 0, r0, c7, c10, 4  @ ARMv5 DSB
  0x04000000 = 67,108,864 iters * ~3 cycles/iter @ 1 GHz ≈ 200 ms
  r0 is scratch here (trashed by __memset; re-loaded by adr_l below).

BSS size on this build: ~1.6 MiB (c12fa628–c133a2d4).
Settle time scales with burst size; 200 ms matches the decompressor fix.

Guarded by #ifdef CONFIG_MACH_SUN5I.

Signed-off-by: SkiffOS <skiffos@skiffos.com>
---
 arch/arm/kernel/head-common.S | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

--- a/arch/arm/kernel/head-common.S
+++ b/arch/arm/kernel/head-common.S
@@ -104,6 +104,21 @@
 	sub	r2, r1, r0
 	mov	r1, #0
 	bl	__memset			@ clear .bss
+#ifdef CONFIG_MACH_SUN5I
+	/*
+	 * sun5i AHB/MBUS bus stall: __memset just zeroed ~1.6 MiB of BSS
+	 * to DRAM in a single burst.  The AHB arbiter retains those write
+	 * transactions; the first post-BSS DRAM read (adr_l init_task below)
+	 * stalls the CPU indefinitely.
+	 * DSB + busy-wait drains the arbiter before any DRAM read.
+	 * r0 is scratch here (trashed by __memset; re-loaded by adr_l below).
+	 */
+	mcr	p15, 0, r0, c7, c10, 4		@ ARMv5 DSB
+	mov	r0, #0x04000000			@ 67M iters ≈ 200ms @ 1 GHz
+sun5i_bss_settle:
+	subs	r0, r0, #1
+	bne	sun5i_bss_settle
+#endif

 	adr_l	r0, init_task			@ get swapper task_struct
 	set_current r0, r1
